> Pi loads AGENTS.md (or CLAUDE.md) at session start and concatenates
> them into the system prompt (per pi docs: "What the model is told →
> AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md / SYSTEM.md"). This file is the persistent
> behavioral instruction surface for the Pi agent.
> Edit the canonical protocol at
> `~/.mpm/agent_installation/mpm-agent-protocol.md` rather than this file
> for behavioral changes.

## MPM behavioral contract — host-independent

MPM is the persistent agent substrate for cross-session continuity on
this machine. The full behavioral contract is canonical at
`~/.mpm/agent_installation/mpm-agent-protocol.md`. The non-negotiable invariants
that this host adapter inherits:

1. **Wake on session start.** Read MPM wake context (`mode`, `persona`,
   active `topics`, recent memories, open work, last handoff) before
   answering substantive questions. With the `pi-mpm` extension
   installed, the wake payload is exposed as the
   `mpm_context` tool's `read_wake_context` action. Calling it is
   mandatory, not optional, at the start of every session.

2. **Persist during work, not only at the end.** Use the appropriate
   MPM tool (`mpm_memory`, `mpm_decisions`, `mpm_lessons`,
   `mpm_topics`, `mpm_references`) for any durable knowledge a future
   session would otherwise have to rediscover. The `pi-mpm` extension
   registers all 16 MPM tools as typed Pi tools; calling them writes
   provenance automatically via the same `mpm call` subprocess
   path that other host plugins use.

3. **Handoff before genuine session closure.** Before sending a turn
   that closes the session, run the handoff check. If substantial work
   has occurred since the last handoff, write it via
   `mpm_session` action `end` (params: `summary`, `state`,
   `commitments`, `open_questions`). Wake is how future-me starts;
   handoff is how future-me receives the previous session.

   Mid-session acknowledgements (`ok`, `thanks`, `ty`, `ack`) are NOT
   session-closing. Do not write a handoff on every chat ack — write
   one when substantial work has happened or a session-closing
   trigger fires.

4. **MPM is the source of truth for cross-session continuity.**
   Anything that should survive across sessions goes to MPM, not just
   transient conversation context.

5. **Recovery / fallback.** If the `pi-mpm` extension fails to load
   or one of the `mpm_*` tools returns an unavailable result, fall
   back to the CLI path:
   `mpm call <tool> --payload '{"action":"<op>","params":{...}}'`
   Do not abandon persistence when the preferred integration breaks.

## Pi-specific notes

Pi searches for AGENTS.md in this order at session start (per Pi docs):

  1. `~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md` (global, all projects)
  2. AGENTS.md in any parent directory of cwd (walking up)
  3. AGENTS.md in cwd (current project)

This file may exist at any of those locations. The installer writes
to the global location (`~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md`) by default for
host-wide behavioral adoption, but accepts `--target` for
project-scoped installation.

The `pi-mpm` extension at `~/.mpm/agent_installation/pi-mpm/` registers
all MPM tools with full coverage. AGENTS.md adds the behavioral
layer (wake, persist, handoff, recovery) that the typed tools alone
do not enforce — capability does not equal adoption.

Disable AGENTS.md loading with `--no-context-files` / `-nc` if you
want to bypass the MPM behavioral contract for a specific session.
